Upgrading Laravel
*****************

.[perex]
Code written for Laravel 5.8 calls `str_slug()` and `array_get()`, which are `Str::slug()` and `Arr::get()` today. The package `dresscode/upgrade-laravel` holds 179 such changes to the framework, from version 6 to the current one, from the helpers through `Request::get()` to the migration columns that version 11 removed. DressCode fixes over a hundred of them right away, and for the rest it says what to write instead. And it offers to write models, commands and jobs with attributes, the way Laravel 13 does.


Installation
============

To upgrade an application that still runs on PHP older than 8.4, install DressCode globally, because DressCode itself needs PHP 8.4 (see [Where DressCode Runs |upgrade#Where DressCode Runs]):

```shell
composer global require dresscode/dresscode dresscode/upgrade-laravel phpstan/phpstan
dresscode upgrade today
```

`upgrade today` finds the data and the rewrites of the package on its own, needs no configuration, and leaves the style of the code as it is. It applies the data only when the project uses `laravel/framework`, and only for the versions the project [has reached |upgrade#Lower Bounds Decide]. [Upgrading Code |upgrade] describes how to go one major version at a time and what to do with what is left. A project that already runs on PHP 8.4 to 8.6 can also add the package as a development dependency, `composer require --dev dresscode/upgrade-laravel phpstan/phpstan`, and then run `vendor/bin/dresscode upgrade today`.

.[note]
Larastan in the project does no harm, quite the opposite: whether you have it in `phpstan.neon` or `phpstan/extension-installer` registered it, DressCode boots the application the same way PHPStan does and reads the facade call `Request::get()` as a method of the class behind it. If `bootstrap/app.php` fails during that, Larastan itself prints the message "Application bootstrap failed" together with the exception that stopped the application from starting, and ends the whole run. Watch out in CI: the run ends with a non-zero exit code, and when it runs in a single process, that code is 1, the same as when DressCode finds something in the code. No static analysis can resolve an alias from `config/app.php` written without a namespace, such as `\Request::get()`, so such a call goes unnoticed.


What Gets Fixed
===============

Here are a middleware, a provider and a controller written for Laravel 10, in a project on Laravel 13:

```php .[before]
use Illuminate\Foundation\Http\Middleware\VerifyCsrfToken as Middleware;  // Class `Illuminate\Foundation\Http\Middleware\VerifyCsrfToken` is replaced by `Illuminate\Foundation\Http\Middleware\PreventRequestForgery`.

class VerifyCsrfToken extends Middleware  // Class `Illuminate\Foundation\Http\Middleware\VerifyCsrfToken` is replaced by `Illuminate\Foundation\Http\Middleware\PreventRequestForgery`.
{
	protected $except = ['stripe/*'];
}


class AppServiceProvider extends ServiceProvider
{
	public function boot(): void
	{
		Schema::useNativeSchemaOperationsIfPossible();  // Static method `Illuminate\Support\Facades\Schema::useNativeSchemaOperationsIfPossible()` is forbidden: there is no replacement; the schema is always changed natively; drop the call.
		URL::forceRootUrl(config('app.url'));  // Static method `Illuminate\Support\Facades\URL::forceRootUrl()` is replaced by `URL::useOrigin()`.
	}
}


class ReportController
{
	public function show(Request $request): array
	{
		$limit = Limit::perMinute(60);
		$limit->decayMinutes = 5;  // Property `Illuminate\Cache\RateLimiting\Limit::$decayMinutes` is replaced by `$this->decaySeconds = $value * 60`.
		return [$request->get('day'), Schema::getAllTables()];  // Method `Illuminate\Http\Request::get()` is replaced by `Request::input()`.
		                                                        // Static method `Illuminate\Support\Facades\Schema::getAllTables()` is forbidden: call `getTables()`, which returns arrays with the name and schema of each table, not rows of the database.
	}
}
```

After `dresscode upgrade today`:

```php .[after]
use Illuminate\Foundation\Http\Middleware\PreventRequestForgery as Middleware;

class VerifyCsrfToken extends Middleware
{
	protected $except = ['stripe/*'];
}


class AppServiceProvider extends ServiceProvider
{
	public function boot(): void
	{
		Schema::useNativeSchemaOperationsIfPossible();
		URL::useOrigin(config('app.url'));
	}
}


class ReportController
{
	public function show(Request $request): array
	{
		$limit = Limit::perMinute(60);
		$limit->decaySeconds = 5 * 60;
		return [$request->input('day'), Schema::getAllTables()];
	}
}
```

The minutes were converted to the seconds Laravel 11 stores in the property. `Schema::getAllTables()` was left alone: `getTables()` returns arrays with the name and schema of each table, not rows of the database, and you have to decide what your code does with them.

Examples of what the package fixes, by the kind of change:

- **Laravel 5.8 helpers**: `str_slug()`, `str_limit()`, `array_get()`, `studly_case()` and 35 others to the methods of `Str` and `Arr` with the same arguments, import included,
- **renamed classes**: `VerifyCsrfToken` and `ValidateCsrfToken` to `PreventRequestForgery`, the trait `HasVersion7Uuids` to `HasUuids`, the facade `Input` to `Request`, `Resource` to `JsonResource`, the testing `TestResponse` and `Assert` to the namespace `Illuminate\Testing`; the import, `extends`, `::class` and `withoutMiddleware()` are all rewritten,
- **renamed members**: `Request::get()` to `input()`, also through the facade, a job's `dispatchNow()` to `dispatchSync()`, `forceRootUrl()` to `useOrigin()`, collection methods such as `containsOneItem()` to `hasSole()`, the property `QueueBusy::$connection` to `$connectionName`, and more,
- **calls of a different shape**: `$limit->decayMinutes` to seconds, `$event->exceptionOccurred` to `$event->exception !== null`, the tests' `assertDeleted()` to `assertModelMissing()`, the migration columns `double('x', 8, 2)` and `unsignedDecimal()` the way Laravel 11 writes them, and more.

The full list is in the data of the package, in the file `upgrading/framework.neon`.

DressCode rewrites a helper called without a leading backslash inside a namespace, as is usual in an application, only with `--fix-risky`: the namespace could declare a function of the same name. The data leaves `str_contains()`, `array_first()` and `array_last()` alone, because they are PHP functions now.

.[note]
`Request::get()` and `input()` are not quite the same: `get()` reads the route attributes first, then the query, and finally the body of the request; `input()` does not read the attributes, puts the body before the query, and takes a key with a dot as a path into an array. Laravel itself recommends `input()` in place of the deprecated `get()`, and DressCode writes it too; read a value that a middleware sets as a route attribute with `$request->attributes->get()`.

Changes in signatures need no data: a method a contract added, or a return type a child class must declare, DressCode reports and writes from the declarations of the installed version. Adding a return type is a [risky fix |configuration#risky-fixes], because the body of the method may return something else.


What Is Left to You
===================

Where there is no replacement, where the replacement returns something else, or where it depends on the database, DressCode reports the place and says what to write. This covers, for example, Doctrine DBAL, which Laravel 11 dropped, or the spatial columns of migrations:

```
Method `Illuminate\Database\Connection::getDoctrineSchemaManager()` is forbidden: read the schema by the schema builder, such as `Schema::getTables()`, `getColumns()` or `getIndexes()`.
Method `Illuminate\Database\Schema\Blueprint::point()` is forbidden: call `geometry()` or `geography()` with the subtype, such as `geography($column, 'point')`; the column type it creates depends on the database.
```

Other places are reported in the same way, for example these:

- **mail**: the move from Swift Mailer to Symfony Mailer (`getSwiftMessage()`, `withSwiftMessage()`, `TransportManager`), and the Mandrill and SparkPost drivers the framework no longer has,
- **tests**: model factories written before Laravel 8, including the helper `factory()`, and the trait `MocksApplicationServices`,
- **queue**: a job's `retryAfter()`, `$retryAfter` and `$timeoutAt`, instead of which the queue reads `backoff()`, `$backoff` and `$retryUntil`,
- **database**: the constructors of grammars and of `Blueprint`, which in Laravel 12 require a connection, `withTablePrefix()`, the column `float()` with the parameters `$total` and `$places`, and the trait `DetectsDeadlocks`,
- **other**: the helper `elixir()`, password validation in `PasswordBroker`, the Rackspace filesystem driver and the trait `DetectsApplicationNamespace`.

Again, the full list, together with what to write instead, is in the data of the package.

What the framework only marks as deprecated, with a note on the replacement, is often not in the data. With the types, DressCode reports it anyway, and where the annotation names the replacement, it rewrites the code. The data does not capture changes in behavior and configuration at all; you will find those in the Laravel upgrade guide.


Keeping the Old API from Coming Back
====================================

To keep the old API from coming back into the code, for example in continuous integration, put the types, the set `deprecations`, and the name of the package into the configuration; the name enables its rewrites in the section `laravel` (see [Upgrading Code |upgrade#Keeping the Old API from Coming Back]):

```neon
typeAnalysis: phpstan

use:
	- perCs           # your standard
	- deprecations
	- upgrade-laravel
```


Models Written the New Way
==========================

Laravel 11 reads the casts of a model from the method `casts()`, Laravel 12.4 recognizes a local scope by the attribute `#[Scope]` instead of the prefix `scope`, and Laravel 13 reads from class attributes what it used to read from properties, for example `#[Table('posts')]` instead of `protected $table = 'posts'`. The old way keeps working, so this is an offer, not a fix. You get it together with the rest of the package data, with the configuration of the previous section:

```neon
typeAnalysis: phpstan

use:
	- perCs           # your standard
	- deprecations
	- upgrade-laravel
```

```php .[before]
class Post extends Model
{
	protected $table = 'blog_posts';  // Property `Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Model::$table` is replaced by the attribute `#[Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Attributes\Table]`.

	public $timestamps = false;  // Property `Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Model::$timestamps` is replaced by the attribute `#[Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Attributes\WithoutTimestamps]`.

	protected $fillable = ['title', 'body'];  // Property `Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Model::$fillable` is replaced by the attribute `#[Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Attributes\Fillable]`.

	protected $casts = ['published_at' => 'datetime'];  // Property `Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Model::$casts` is replaced by the method `casts()`.


	public function scopePublished(Builder $query): void  // Method `scopePublished()` is replaced by the method `published()` with the attribute `#[Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Attributes\Scope]`.
	{
		$query->whereNotNull('published_at');
	}
}
```

After `dresscode fix --fix-risky`:

```php .[after]
#[WithoutTimestamps]
#[Table(name: 'blog_posts')]
#[Fillable(['title', 'body'])]
class Post extends Model
{
	protected function casts(): array
	{
		return ['published_at' => 'datetime'];
	}


	#[Scope]
	protected function published(Builder $query): void
	{
		$query->whereNotNull('published_at');
	}
}
```

Properties that map to the same attribute class, `$table`, `$primaryKey` and `$keyType`, are merged into a single `#[Table]`; the page [Upgrading to Laravel 13 |upgrade-to-laravel-13#Laravel 13 Attributes] lists which attributes the data knows for models, commands, jobs and other classes. Rewriting the scope is a risky fix, so it needs `--fix-risky`: DressCode cannot see a call to `scopePublished()` by its old name in another file. It makes the method `published()` protected, because otherwise the static call `Post::published()` would not reach it.

.[note]
The decision `laravel.castsMethod` from `laravel/framework` 11 writes the method `casts()`, and `laravel.scopeAttribute` from 12.4 writes a scope with an attribute. The data itself turns them on, and the plugin that the name of the package in `use` enables checks them; if you do not want one of them, turn it off in the section `laravel` with the value `keep`.


What the Package Covers
=======================

| what | versions |
|---|---|
| helpers `str_*` and `array_*` | 6 |
| deprecated and removed API of the framework | 6 to [13 |upgrade-to-laravel-13] |
| attributes instead of properties | [13 |upgrade-to-laravel-13] |
| `casts()` instead of `$casts` | 11 |
| `#[Scope]` instead of the prefix `scope` | 12.4 |

The package starts with Laravel 6, so it upgrades code written for Laravel 5.8; from there, go one major version at a time, as described in [Upgrading Code |upgrade#One Major Version at a Time, by Hand]. It has no data for versions older than 5.8, and the move to 5.8 is yours to do by hand.

